let's talk about the ad hominem fallacy  ad hominem is short for argumentum ad  hominem which is Latin for argument  against the man or argument against the  person and that's what we're talking  about an ad hominem attack is an attack  on a person making an argument often to  avoid addressing the argument itself  this is another example of an informal  fallacy because ad hominem is a type of  fallacy that deals with the content of  an argument not its form let's say that  John is making an argument and he says  artificial intelligence has the  potential to become smarter than humans  this type of AI could become difficult  for humans to control humanity should  have existential concerns about the  future of AI John's friend Steve  responds by saying John you're just  paranoid that's ridiculous  here Steve is also making an argument in  order to refute what John is saying  Steve's argument is John is making a  claim there's something objectionable  about John John's claim is false this is  an invalid argument John's character  which is to say whether or not John is a  paranoid person has no bearing on his  argument about artificial intelligence  and this is why it's important to  understand the ad hominem fallacy even  if John is what could be described as a  paranoid person this is irrelevant to  the soundness of his argument here about  AI there are several different types of  ad hominem fallacy x' Steve's attack on  John was an example of an abusive ad  hominem and this is how most people  think of ad hominem xan abusive ad  hominem is an attempt to refute someone  by saying something bad about them  another type is the circumstantial ad  hominem this is when someone tries to  refute that claim based on what  consequences that claim might have for  the person making the claim in this case  a person's perceived motivations or  interests are being invoked despite  those things having no relevance to the  soundness of that person's argument  here's an example this guy is a  fundamentalist Christian so we can't  trust what he says about evolution not  being real  I'm fallaciously attacking his  circumstance as a Christian and his  claims about evolution do indeed warrant  their own evaluation and criticism  poisoning the well is a type of ad  hominem where you fallaciously try to  refute someone's claim before the claim  is even made poisoning the well is the  use of an ad hominem before the  disagreement has even been established  in an attempt to discredit one's  opponent oh look Jake Tapper is on TV I  wonder what he's saying probably just  lies everyone knows he's just fake news  guilt by association is another type of  ad hominem also known as the association  fallacy this is where you try to refute  someone's argument simply by associating  them with other people or things that  are negative X is a why X is also a Z  therefore all Y's are Z's Stalin was an  atheist Stalin killed millions of people  atheism is evil another type of ad  hominem attacking a person on the basis  of their inconsistencies  for instance saying that a person's  argument is wrong because they used to  argue the opposite position or saying  that someone is wrong because their  actions don't conform to what they're  arguing this candidates stance on this  issue doesn't make any sense because he  used to have a different stance or this  guy's arguments about red meat being  unhealthy can't be valid because he eats  cheeseburgers but I will add it's not  necessarily fallacious to simply call  someone a hypocrite or  to claim that someone is wishy-washy  about their position on something by  appealing to the inconsistencies of the  behavior or conclusions for example if  someone is very easily swayed by the  opinions of others there may be room to  soundly argue that this person doesn't  have a firm grasp on what they believe  and this is an important point an attack  on someone's character is not always an  ad hominem ad hominem Zoar attacks on a  pers